\section developer_backend_id_class_sec Backend Identifier Classes

A "backend identifier class" is a simple type of class that is designed 
to be used a template argument whose primary purpose is to identify a 
choice of computational backend.  In PSCF, classes that are defined to
use a specific computational backend are defined as specializations of 
class templates that accept a special type of "backend identifier class" 
as a template argument. 

PSCF currently defines such backend identifier classes, named Pscf::CPT 
and Pscf::CUT, for which:

  - Pscf::CPT is the identifier class for the C++ CPU backend

  - Pscf::CUT is the identifier class for the CUDA GPU backend

Specializations of class and function templates for which Pscf::CPT 
is a template argument thus use the C++ backend, and corresponding
template specializations for which Pscf:CUT is a template argument 
instead use the CUDA GPU backend.

\section developer_backend_id_rp_sec Template Patterns

The Pscf and Pscf::Rp namespaces contain many class templates that 
take a backend identifier class as a template parameter. Many of these 
templates also take an additional int template parameter, denoted by D, 
that represents the integer dimension of space. For such classes, the 
dimension of space D generally appears as the first template parameter, 
and a backend identifier class parameter, denoted by T, generally 
appears as a second parameter.  

<b> Example </b>:
The Pscf::Rp::System class template represents a complete physical system, 
and is used to construct the highest level object in the ownership
hierarchy used by pscf_rpc or pscf_rpg. This template is declared as:
\code
namespace Pscf {
   namespace Rp {
      template <int D, class T> class System;
   }
}
\endcode
A valid specialization of this template must take an integer argument 
D=1, 2, or 3, and must take one of the two possible template identifier 
classes as the argument of parameter T.  The template instantiation
\code
namespace Pscf {
   Pscf::Rp::System <3, CUT>
}
\endcode
is thus a specialization of the Pscf::Rp::System class template that is 
designed to use a CUDA backend to treat systems with three-dimensional 
(D=3) periodic boundary conditions.

Almost all of the class templates defined in the Pscf::Rp namespace take
these same template parameters, with the same meaning.

\section developer_backend_id_enum_sec BackendId Enum Class

The Pscf namespace also defines an enum class named Pscf::BackendId for 
which different enum values also identify different backends.  This enum 
class is defined in the file src/pscf/backend/BackendId.h, as follows:
\code
namespace Pscf {

   enum class BackendId {
      Cpp, Cuda
   };

}
\endcode
The enum value Pscf::BackendId::Cpp represents the C++  CPU backend, while 
Pscf::BackendId::Cuda represents the CUDA GPU-enabled backend.

The backend identifier classes Pscf::CPT and Pscf::CUT each define a const 
static member variable named "id" that is an instance of the BackendId 
enum class. This static variable is assigned the appropriate constant value 
in each backend identifier class.  The definition of the class Pscf::CUT 
thus reads, in part
\code
namespace Pscf {

    class CUT {
       
        static const BackendId id = BackendId::Cuda;

        // ...
        
    };

}
\endcode
The corresponding definition of the class Pscf::CPT instead contains a 
member declaration "static const BackendId id = BackendId::Cpp".

Each backend identifier class and each value of the BackendId enum are 
both designed to identify a choice of backend, but are used in different 
contexts. A backend identifier class is used at compile time, as a 
template argument that identifies a template specialization that uses
uses a particular backend. A value of type BackendId can instead be 
tested at run time. Specifically, within a class or function template 
in which the template parameter name T represents a backend identifier 
class, the value of T::id can be tested to identify the backend at run 
time.

<BR>
